“I didn’t put my name in that goblet!” said Harry, starting to feel angry.
“Yeah, okay,” said Ron, in exactly the same skeptical tone as Cedric. “Only
you said this morning you’d have done it last night, and no one would’ve seen
you. . . . I’m not stupid, you know.”
“You’re doing a really good impression of it,” Harry snapped.
“Yeah?” said Ron, and there was no trace of a grin, forced or otherwise, on
his face now. “You want to get to bed, Harry. I expect you’ll need to be up
early tomorrow for a photo-call or something.”
He wrenched the hangings shut around his four-poster, leaving Harry
standing there by the door, staring at the dark red velvet curtains, now hiding
one of the few people he had been sure would believe him.
